Section 50 gives every person ten duties:

- (1) to protect the nation, religions, the King and the democratic regime of government with the King as Head of State;
- (2) to defend the country, protect the nation's honour and interests and public property, and cooperate in preventing and relieving disasters;
- (3) to obey the law strictly;
- (4) to attend compulsory education;
- (5) to serve in the armed forces as the law provides;
- (6) to respect and not violate the rights and freedoms of others, and not do anything that may cause division or hatred in society;
- (7) to vote in elections and referendums freely, with the common interest of the country as the main consideration;
- (8) to cooperate in and support conservation of the environment, natural resources, biodiversity and cultural heritage;
- (9) to pay taxes as the law provides;
- (10) not to take part in or support corruption or misconduct of any kind.

The ten fall into three groups: protecting the country (1, 2, 5), everyday good citizenship (3, 4, 6, 9), and duties that are new or newly stressed (7 voting, 8 environment, 10 anti-corruption).

Section 54 is one of the longest in Chapter 5. Its core is the first sentence: the state must ensure every child receives twelve years of quality education, from pre-school until the end of compulsory education, free of charge.

The rest of section 54:
- the state must care for and develop young children before school, physically, mentally, in discipline, emotions, social skills and intellect, with local government and the private sector taking part;
- the state must give people education that suits their needs in different systems and promote lifelong learning;
- there must be a national education law that at least provides for a national education plan and for monitoring its implementation;
- all education must aim to make learners good people, disciplined, proud of the nation, skilled in their aptitudes, and responsible to family, community, society and country;
- a fund must be set up to support those without means, reduce inequality and develop teachers, and the fund must be managed independently.

The fund became real as the Equitable Education Fund, under the Equitable Education Fund Act of 2018.

Why count from pre-school? Because a large body of child-development research finds the early years are where investment pays off most, and the gap between rich and poor children opens before they even reach first grade.

Section 62 requires the state to keep strict fiscal discipline so that public finances remain stable and sustainable, under a fiscal discipline law, and to make the tax system fair. That law must at least cover the fiscal and budget framework, discipline over revenue and spending both inside and outside the budget, management of state assets and the treasury, and public debt. The law that followed is the State Fiscal and Financial Disciplines Act of 2018.

Picture a family where everyone carries a card on the household account. Without rules on how much each person may spend and whom they must tell first, someone eventually buys something that makes them look good this month and leaves the bill for next year's household. Governments are the same. A government lasts four years; its debt lasts far longer. Fiscal discipline is a rule that protects future governments from the present one.

The fiscal discipline chain across the constitution:
- section 62: the state must keep fiscal discipline and have a fiscal discipline law;
- section 140: public money may be spent only as budget or fiscal laws allow;
- section 142: the budget bill must show its revenue sources, expected results and consistency with the national strategy;
- section 162: the cabinet must explain where the money for its policies will come from when it presents its policy statement;
- section 164(2): the cabinet must strictly follow the fiscal discipline law in handling public money;
- section 258 ก.(3): political reform must include a mechanism making parties answerable for announcing policies without analysing their impact, value for money and risks.

The effect: every big spending policy must answer one question from campaign day to payment day: “where does the money come from?”

Section 63 requires the state to promote, support and educate the public about the dangers of corruption in both the public and private sectors; to have effective measures and mechanisms to prevent and eliminate corruption strictly; and to have mechanisms that encourage citizens to organise campaigns, resist corruption or blow the whistle, with protection from the state.

The 2017 charter places corruption in several layers on purpose:
- the citizen layer: section 50(10), do not take part in or support corruption;
- the state layer: section 63, prevent and eliminate it, and protect whistleblowers;
- the policy layer: section 76, the state should develop honest officials, a merit system for personnel, and ethical standards that agencies use to write their codes of ethics;
- the participation layer: section 78, the state should encourage people to take part in scrutinising state power and fighting corruption.

Why so many layers? Because corruption needs two sides, a giver and a taker. Rules that only catch the official leave the giver with every incentive. If whistleblowers are not protected, no one speaks. The rules have to cover every corner.

There is history behind it. The 2017 drafters named the prevention and suppression of corruption as one of the charter's main goals, which links this section to the design of the independent organs and the ethical standards in other chapters.

Section 65 sits in the directive-principles chapter and opens with “the State should have a national strategy” as the goal for sustainable national development under good governance, and as a framework that keeps all plans consistent. Its last paragraph then changes the weight entirely: “once published in the Royal Gazette, the national strategy shall be enforceable.”

This is the point to grasp. Having a national strategy is a “should”. Once published, the strategy binds, and the constitution wires it into several other mechanisms:
- section 65, paragraph 2: the process is set by law, and that law must provide for broad public participation and hearings from every sector;
- section 142: the budget bill must show its consistency with the national strategy;
- section 162: the cabinet's policies must be consistent with the national strategy;
- section 270: the first, transitional Senate was charged with monitoring, advising on and speeding up reform and the implementation of the national strategy;
- section 275: the cabinet had to pass the section 65 law within 120 days of the constitution's promulgation and complete the national strategy within one year of that law taking effect.

The law that followed is the National Strategy Preparation Act of 2017. The National Strategy (2018–2037) was published in the Royal Gazette on 13 October 2018: a 20-year plan in six areas, namely security; competitiveness; human-capital development; social opportunity and equality; environmentally friendly growth; and rebalancing and developing public-sector management. It may be reviewed every five years or when circumstances change.

The preparation act also builds in enforcement. As iLaw summarises it, if a state agency fails to comply and does not correct course, the matter can reach the National Anti-Corruption Commission and lead to penalties for the agency head; if non-compliance stems from a cabinet resolution, the path runs through the Senate, the Constitutional Court, the NACC and the Supreme Court's Criminal Division for Holders of Political Positions.

The full chain: the published strategy binds → budgets and government policies must align with it → there are mechanisms to monitor and sanction → so any government that wins a future election can only govern within this 20-year frame.
